/* Built by tools/build-app-screens.mjs. Edit tools/app-screens/src, not this file. */
window.APP_SCREENS=[{"id":"analytics-dashboard","t":"Analytics dashboard","cat":"Dashboards","tone":"light","d":"Four headline numbers, a traffic chart you can read point by point, top pages and where visitors come from, with a date range switch.","how":"All figures come from one data object per range. Switching range redraws the KPI cards, the SVG line chart and the tables from that object. The chart is drawn by a 40-line function with a hover and arrow-key readout, so there is no chart library to load.","use":"Product analytics, marketing reports, SaaS home pages, admin overviews.","p":"Build an analytics dashboard as one HTML file with no libraries. App shell: a left sidebar with a logo, ten nav links and the signed-in user, a top bar with search, a theme switch and notifications, and a scrolling content area; under 860px the sidebar becomes an off-canvas menu. Content: a heading with a 7 days / 30 days / 90 days segmented control and an Export button; four KPI cards (visitors, sign-ups, conversion, average session) each with a value, a change badge that says up or down in words and a sparkline; a wide card with an SVG line chart comparing this period with the previous one (dashed), with gridlines, axis labels, a hover guide and tooltip, and arrow-key support when focused; a Top pages table with a share bar per row; and a Sources list with percentage bars. Keep all data in one object keyed by range and re-render everything when the range changes. Define colours as CSS variables with a dark set under html[data-theme=dark]."},
{"id":"podcast-player","t":"Podcast player app","cat":"Mobile apps","tone":"light","added":"2026-11","d":"A phone-sized podcast player on a dark stage: large cover art, a scrubber you can drag, skip back and forward, playback speed, chapters you can jump to, a queue you can reorder and a library of shows to follow.","how":"One timer moves the position forward while playing, scaled by the speed. The scrubber, the time labels and the highlighted chapter all read that one number, so dragging the bar or tapping a chapter only has to set it. The queue is an array; moving an episode swaps two items and redraws.","use":"Podcast, music and audiobook apps, meditation and language-lesson players, any phone screen built around playback.","p":"Build a mobile podcast player as one HTML file with no libraries for an app called Earshot, shown inside a phone frame on wide screens and full width on phones. The app is dark in both themes. Bottom tab bar with Player, Queue and Library (role=tablist, arrow keys). Player: a small header with the show name, large square cover art (a photograph) with a soft shadow, the episode title and show, a scrubber (input type=range) with elapsed and remaining time, a row of controls (back 15 seconds, play or pause as the large round button, forward 30 seconds), a second row with a speed button that cycles 1x, 1.25x, 1.5x and 2x, a sleep timer toggle and a share button, then a Chapters list with start times where the current chapter is marked and tapping one jumps to it. Playback is simulated with a timer that respects the speed. Queue: Now playing, then Up next as a list with cover thumbnails, durations and up and down buttons to reorder; tapping an episode plays it. Library: a two-column grid of followed shows with cover, name, a new-episodes count and a Following toggle. Show a mini player above the tab bar on the Queue and Library tabs. Colours are CSS variables."},
{"id":"sales-dashboard","t":"Sales dashboard","cat":"Dashboards","tone":"light","d":"Revenue against target, a monthly bar chart, a channel donut, recent orders with status and a best-sellers list.","how":"The target ring and the donut are SVG circles whose dash lengths are computed from the numbers, so they redraw from data. Orders are an array rendered into a table with status badges; the status filter narrows the same array.","use":"E-commerce back offices, shop owners, sales teams, marketplace sellers.","p":"Build a sales dashboard as one HTML file with no libraries, for a shop back office called Fernhill. Shell: one top bar holding the logo, six navigation links (Home, Orders with a count, Products, Customers, Discounts, Reports) with an underline on the current one, a search field, a theme switch, notifications and the account avatar; content sits in a centred column up to 1200px wide; under 860px the links move to a second, sideways-scrolling row. Content: a greeting heading with a New order button; a row of three cards: revenue this month on a dark inverted card with a progress ring towards a target, orders with average order value, and refunds; a bar chart of revenue by month with the current month highlighted; a donut chart of sales by channel with a legend that lists each share as text; a Recent orders table (order number, customer with initials avatar, items, total, status badge, date) with an All / Paid / Pending / Refunded filter; and a Best sellers list with a small bar for stock left. Draw the ring and donut with SVG circles and stroke-dasharray computed from the data. Colours are CSS variables with a dark theme."},
{"id":"support-desk","t":"Support inbox","cat":"Communication","tone":"light","added":"2026-11","d":"A help desk in four columns: an icon rail, a queue with reply-time warnings, the conversation with replies and private notes, and the customer's details and orders.","how":"Conversations are one array. The queue, the counts on the filter and the thread are all drawn from it, so sending a reply, adding a note or changing the status updates every column at once. Saved replies are a small menu that writes into the reply box.","use":"Customer support and help desk tools, shared team inboxes, live chat consoles, moderation queues.","p":"Build a support inbox as one HTML file with no libraries for a help desk called Relay. Keep it dense: 13px text and tight rows. Four columns: (1) a 56px icon rail on a dark inverted surface with the logo, five icon links with tooltips, a theme switch and the agent's avatar; (2) a 300px queue with a heading, an Open / Waiting / Closed filter showing counts, a search field and a list (role=listbox, arrow keys) where each row has the customer's initials, name, time, subject, a one-line preview, a channel label and a reply-due badge that turns amber then red; (3) the conversation: subject, a status select, an Assign to me button, then customer messages on the left, agent replies on the right in the accent colour and private notes on an amber tint with a label, and a composer with a Reply / Note switch, a Saved replies menu that inserts text, and Send; (4) a 280px customer panel with contact details, lifetime figures, recent orders with status badges and earlier conversations. Sending a reply or changing the status must update the queue and its counts. Under 1200px hide the customer panel; under 860px show the queue first and open a conversation full width with a Back button. Colours are CSS variables with a dark theme."},
{"id":"stay-search","t":"Travel search with a map","cat":"Pages","tone":"light","added":"2026-11","d":"Search results for places to stay: photo cards on the left, a drawn map with price pins on the right, linked both ways, with filters, sorting, a price limit and saved favourites.","how":"Stays are one array. Filters and the sort order produce the visible list, and both the cards and the pins are drawn from it, so they can never disagree. The map is an SVG drawing with the pins as real buttons placed by percentage, which keeps them reachable by keyboard.","use":"Travel and property search, restaurant and venue finders, store locators, any results list that needs a map beside it.","p":"Build a travel search results page as one HTML file with no libraries for a booking product called Wayfarer. Top bar: logo, a search summary made of three joined fields (place, dates, guests) with a round search button, a theme switch and an avatar. Under it a filter row: pill toggles for Entire place, Free cancellation and Breakfast included, a maximum price slider with its value, a sort select (Recommended, Price low to high, Rating) and a result count. Main area in two panes: a scrolling list of result cards (photo on the left with a heart button to save, then title, area and type, a star rating with the number of reviews, feature badges, price per night and the total for the stay) and a map that fills the rest. Draw the map in SVG: land, a river, parks, main roads and district names, with no map library, and scale it with plus and minus buttons. Each stay has a pin: a pill button showing its nightly price, positioned by percentage. Hovering or focusing a card highlights its pin and the reverse; choosing a pin scrolls its card into view and opens a small summary card on the map. Filters, the slider and sorting update cards, pins and the count together, with an empty state. Under 900px show the list with a floating Map button that swaps to the map. Colours are CSS variables with a dark theme."},
{"id":"ops-monitor","t":"Operations monitor","cat":"Dashboards","tone":"light","d":"A live status board: service health, latency and error charts that keep moving, regional status and a streaming event log you can pause.","how":"A timer adds one reading a second to rolling arrays and redraws the SVG charts from them. Service rows carry their own sparkline and a status computed from thresholds. The log is a capped list in a polite live region that stops updating while paused.","use":"Infrastructure status, DevOps tools, IoT fleets, call-centre wallboards, trading and logistics screens.","p":"Build a live operations monitor as one HTML file with no libraries, for a product called Beacon, with light and dark themes. Shell: a 56px icon rail on a dark inverted surface (logo, six icon links with tooltips and an alert dot, theme switch, avatar) and a slim header with a breadcrumb, search and an environment badge. Keep it dense: 13px text, tight gaps, monospace figures. Layout: the metric tiles as one joined strip, then a wide column (the two charts side by side above the Services table) beside a 330px column (region cards in a two by two grid above the event log). Content: a header with an overall status pill (All systems normal / Degraded), a Live / Paused toggle button and a time range label; four metric tiles (requests per second, p95 latency, error rate, uptime) that update every second; two SVG line charts (latency and error rate) drawn from rolling 60-point arrays; a Services table with name, status dot and word, a sparkline, latency and a region; a row of region cards with a status bar; and an event log that prepends a line every few seconds with a timestamp, level badge and message, capped at 40 lines, inside aria-live=polite. Pausing stops the timer. Respect prefers-reduced-motion by updating every five seconds instead. All colours are CSS variables."},
{"id":"doc-editor","t":"Document editor with comments","cat":"Work & projects","tone":"light","added":"2026-11","d":"A calm writing screen: a page you can type on, a small formatting bar, an outline built from the headings, and comments in the margin tied to highlighted text, with replies and resolve.","how":"The page is one editable element. The outline and the word count are rebuilt from it as you type. A comment wraps the selected words in a mark and adds a card beside the page; the two carry the same number, so pointing at either lights the other and resolving removes both.","use":"Writing and note tools, knowledge bases, contract and policy review, any long text that people discuss.","p":"Build a document editor with margin comments as one HTML file with no libraries for a writing product called Folio. Keep the chrome quiet and the page airy. Top bar: a back arrow, the document title as an input, a saved status, three overlapping collaborator avatars, a Comments toggle with a count, a theme switch and a Share button. Under it a centred pill toolbar: a paragraph style select (Text, Heading, Subheading), bold, italic, underline, bulleted list, quote and an Add comment button that is enabled only while text in the page is selected. Three columns: an outline (list of the page's headings, click to scroll, rebuilt as headings change) and a word count with reading time; a paper page up to 720px wide with generous padding and serif body text, editable in place; and a comments column where each card shows the author, time, the quoted words, the comment, replies, a reply field and a Resolve button. Commented words are highlighted in the page; hovering or focusing a card or its highlight emphasises both. Adding a comment wraps the selection, opens a new card and focuses its field. Under 1100px hide the outline; under 860px the comments become a slide-in panel opened by the toggle. Colours are CSS variables with a dark theme."},
{"id":"recipe-app","t":"Recipe app","cat":"Mobile apps","tone":"light","added":"2026-11","d":"A phone-sized cooking app: a recipe with a full-width photograph, servings that rescale every quantity, ingredients you tick off, steps with their own countdown timers, and a shopping list that fills from what you have not got.","how":"Quantities are stored for one serving and multiplied when drawn, with small rules for halves and plurals. Ticked ingredients are a set; whatever is not in it can be sent to the shopping list, which is another array shown on its own tab. Each step timer is a countdown that writes into its own button.","use":"Recipe and meal-planning apps, grocery lists, how-to guides with timed steps, any checklist that scales with a quantity.","p":"Build a mobile recipe app as one HTML file with no libraries for an app called Larder, shown inside a phone frame on wide screens and full width on phones. Bottom tab bar with Recipes, Cook and Shopping (role=tablist, arrow keys); Shopping shows a count badge. Cook (the default) is one recipe: a photograph across the top with round Back and Save buttons over it, then on a sheet that overlaps the photo the title, a line of facts (time, difficulty, rating), a Serves stepper from 1 to 8, and an Ingredients / Method switch. Ingredients are checkboxes with the quantity on the right; quantities rescale with the servings (grams and millilitres rounded to 5, halves written as fractions, plurals handled); ticked rows are struck through, and a button adds every unticked ingredient to the shopping list. Method is a numbered list; steps that need it have a timer button that counts down in place, can be stopped, and announces when it finishes. Recipes: a list of cards with a photo, title, time and a saved heart, opening that recipe. Shopping: the collected items as checkboxes grouped under the recipe name, with Clear ticked. Colours are CSS variables with a dark theme."},
{"id":"week-planner","t":"Calendar, week view","cat":"Work & projects","tone":"light","added":"2026-11","d":"A week calendar with a time grid: events sized by their length, overlaps side by side, a line at the current time, calendars you can hide, a mini month and a quick form to add an event.","how":"Every event is a start and an end in hours. Its top and height are those numbers times the height of an hour, and events that overlap are put in columns so they share the day. The mini month and the header are computed from one date, so the arrows move both.","use":"Scheduling tools, booking and rota screens, meeting planners, timetable views inside any product.","p":"Build a week calendar as one HTML file with no libraries for a product called Tempo. Shell: a single top bar with the logo, a Today button, previous and next arrows, the date range as the title, a Day / Week switch, a theme switch and a New event button; no sidebar navigation. Left column (220px): a mini month with the visible week tinted and today circled, and a My calendars list of four checkboxes with coloured dots that show or hide their events. Main: a header row of seven days (today's number in a filled circle), an all-day row, then a time grid from 08:00 to 20:00 with hour lines and a 56px time gutter. Events are absolutely positioned buttons (top and height from start and end times), tinted by calendar with a 3px left rule; overlapping events sit side by side. Draw a line with a dot at the current time on today's column. Clicking an event opens a small details card with a Delete button; clicking an empty slot or New event opens a short form (title, day, start, length, calendar) that adds the event. Day view shows one column. Under 900px hide the left column; under 700px let the grid scroll sideways inside its own frame. Colours are CSS variables with a dark theme."},
{"id":"video-call","t":"Video call","cat":"Communication","tone":"light","added":"2026-11","d":"A meeting screen with no app chrome: a shared screen with a strip of people, or an even grid, a ring on whoever is speaking, working mic, camera, hand and layout controls, and a chat and people panel.","how":"People are an array with mic, camera and hand flags; the tiles, the people list and the count are drawn from it. A timer picks the next person who is not muted as the speaker. Each control flips a flag and redraws, so muting yourself shows on your tile and in the list at once.","use":"Meeting and webinar products, telehealth and tutoring, live support, any screen built around video tiles.","p":"Build a video call screen as one HTML file with no libraries for a meeting product called Huddle. No sidebar or navigation: the whole window is a dark stage in both themes. Top row: the meeting title, a running timer, a REC badge, then a Speaker / Grid layout switch, a people count and a theme switch. Stage: in Speaker layout a large tile showing a shared screen (draw a simple slide with HTML and CSS) and a column of smaller tiles; in Grid layout six equal tiles. A tile shows the person's initials in a tinted circle (no photos), a name label with a muted-mic icon when muted, a raised-hand badge, and a green ring with small moving level bars while that person speaks. Bottom: a centred control bar with Mic, Camera, Share, Raise hand, Chat and a red Leave button; toggles use aria-pressed and turn red when off. A 320px side panel with Chat and People tabs: chat messages with a field that adds yours, and a people list with mic state and a Mute all button. Leaving shows a short summary with Rejoin. Every few seconds move the speaker to another unmuted person. Under 860px the panel covers the stage and the strip runs sideways. Colours are CSS variables."},
{"id":"bank-transfer","t":"Banking, with a transfer flow","cat":"Dashboards","tone":"light","added":"2026-11","d":"Online banking on the web: a dark band with the total and three accounts, a searchable list of transactions, and a three-step payment (details, review, done) that checks the amount and updates the balances.","how":"Accounts and transactions are plain arrays. The total is the sum of the accounts, so a payment that leaves the bank lowers it and a move between your own accounts does not. The transfer form validates as you go, shows a review step, then writes the new transaction and redraws everything.","use":"Banking and fintech dashboards, wallets, payroll and expense tools, any screen that moves money and needs a careful confirm step.","p":"Build an online banking overview with a transfer flow as one HTML file with no libraries for a bank called Harbour. Top bar: logo, pill-style navigation (Accounts, Payments, Cards, Savings, Help), a last sign-in note, a theme switch and an avatar. Below it a full-width dark inverted band: a greeting, the total balance in large type with a show or hide button, a 30-day balance sparkline, and three account cards (current account, bills pot, savings with its rate), one selected. Content in a centred column up to 1120px, two columns: on the left the selected account's transactions grouped by day, each with a category icon, payee, note and signed amount (money in is green), with an All / In / Out filter and a search field; on the right a Move money card that works in three steps with a progress line. Step 1: From account, To (saved payees or one of your other accounts), amount in pounds with quick amounts, and a reference; validate that the amount is above zero and within the balance and say so in words. Step 2: a review list of the details with Back and Confirm. Step 3: a tick, what was sent and the new balance, with Make another payment. Confirming adds the transaction to the list and updates the account cards and the total. Under the card list two scheduled payments. Under 900px stack the columns. Colours are CSS variables with a dark theme."},
{"id":"issue-tracker","t":"Issue list with a command palette","cat":"Work & projects","tone":"light","added":"2026-11","d":"A dense issue tracker driven from the keyboard: a grouped list with priority, status and labels, and a command palette that searches issues and runs actions such as change status, assign, filter or create.","how":"Issues are one array and the list is grouped from it on every change. The palette is a filtered list of commands; some open a second step (pick a status, a person, a label) that edits the selected issue. Arrow keys move a highlighted row in both the list and the palette, so nothing needs the mouse.","use":"Issue and task trackers, admin tools for expert users, any product where a command menu is the fastest way around.","p":"Build an issue tracker with a command palette as one HTML file with no libraries for a product called Linea. Dense and quiet: 13px text, 36px rows, almost no chrome. Header: a small logo, a breadcrumb, a wide button that reads Search or run a command with a Ctrl K hint, a theme switch and an avatar. A second row has Active / All tabs with counts, a removable label filter chip and a New issue button. The list is grouped by status (In progress, In review, Todo, Done) with collapsible group headings and counts; each row shows a priority icon (bars, or a red mark for urgent), a monospace id, a status ring drawn in SVG, the title, a label badge, the assignee's initials and a date. One row is selected at a time (click, or J and K and the arrow keys). A footer bar lists the shortcuts. The palette opens with Ctrl or Cmd K or the slash key: an input, then results in groups (Actions, Issues, Go to) filtered as you type, moved through with the arrow keys and run with Enter. Actions: Create issue (asks for a title), Change status, Assign to, Set priority and Filter by label (each opens a second list of options for the selected issue), Switch theme and Copy link. Escape steps back, then closes. Single-key shortcuts C, S, A, P and F open the matching step. Colours are CSS variables with a dark theme."},
{"id":"pos-till","t":"Point of sale till","cat":"Mobile apps","tone":"light","added":"2026-11","d":"A tablet till for a bakery counter: big photo tiles by category, an order ticket with quantity steppers, a loyalty discount, VAT worked out, and card or cash payment with change.","how":"The order is a map of product to quantity. Tapping a tile adds one, and the ticket, the badge on each tile and the totals are all drawn from that map. The cash sheet takes the amount handed over and shows the change before it lets you finish the sale.","use":"Shop and café tills, kiosk and self-order screens, market stalls, ticket desks, any touch screen that builds a basket.","p":"Build a point of sale till as one HTML file with no libraries, shown inside a landscape tablet frame (1180 by 820) on wide screens and full width on a real tablet. Touch-sized targets throughout. Top bar: the shop name and till number, a clock, an Online badge, a theme switch and the member of staff. Left: a row of category pills (All, Coffee, Pastries, Bread, Lunch) and a grid of product tiles, each a photograph with the name and price beneath and a count badge once it is in the order; tapping a tile adds one. Right (360px): the order ticket with an order number, an Eat in / Take away switch, lines with minus and plus steppers and line totals, an empty state, a Loyalty 10% switch, then subtotal, discount, VAT included (a sixth of the total) and the total in large type, with Card and Cash buttons. Card shows a short waiting state then a paid receipt. Cash opens a sheet with quick amounts (exact, the next £5, £10, £20) that shows the change due and a Take payment button. After payment show Paid with the change and a New order button that clears the ticket and moves the order number on. Under 760px the ticket sits below the products. Colours are CSS variables with a dark theme."},
{"id":"report-page","t":"Quarterly report","cat":"Dashboards","tone":"light","added":"2026-11","d":"A report laid out like printed pages: a headline finding, four figures with their change, a labelled line chart, revenue by plan as stacked bars with a table, a retention grid and notes. Switch the quarter, or print it.","how":"Each quarter is one data object. The headline, figures, both charts and both tables are written from it, and the totals in the table are added up in code so they always match the bars. A print stylesheet hides the toolbar and breaks the pages cleanly.","use":"Board and investor updates, client reports, monthly summaries, anything read on paper or as a PDF rather than watched live.","p":"Build a quarterly customer report as one HTML file with no libraries for an analytics product called Meridian. It should read like a printed document, not a dashboard: a soft grey desk, and white pages up to 880px wide with wide margins, a serif headline and thin rules instead of cards. A slim toolbar above the pages has the logo, a breadcrumb, a quarter select, a theme switch, Print (window.print) and Download PDF. Page 1: an eyebrow line with the period, a large headline stating the main finding, a standfirst paragraph, a row of four figures separated by rules (active customers, new sign-ups, customers lost, revenue), each with a change against the previous quarter in words, then Figure 1: an SVG line chart of active customers over twelve months with the quarter shaded, the first and last points labelled directly and a caption; then two columns of short text, What changed and What we will do next. Page 2: Figure 2, revenue by plan as three stacked horizontal bars (one per month) with a legend, beside a table of the same numbers with a total row computed in code; Figure 3, a retention grid where each cell is tinted by its percentage; and numbered notes on method. Each page ends with a footer line and page number. Changing the quarter rewrites everything from one data object. Add print styles: no toolbar, no shadows, one sheet per page. Colours are CSS variables with a dark theme."},
{"id":"crm-pipeline","t":"CRM pipeline","cat":"Dashboards","tone":"light","d":"A sales pipeline: open value, weighted forecast and win rate, a funnel by stage, a deals table where changing a stage updates every number, and a team activity feed.","how":"Deals are one array and every figure is derived from it: the funnel bars, the open total and the forecast (each deal's value times the chance of its stage). Changing a deal's stage in the table edits the array and redraws, so the bars slide to their new widths.","use":"Sales teams, agencies tracking proposals, recruiting pipelines, fundraising trackers.","p":"Build a CRM pipeline dashboard as one HTML file with no libraries, for a sales tool called Tally. Shell: a dark inverted header in two rows, the first with the logo, a workspace switcher, search, a theme switch and the avatar, the second with six section links (Pipeline, Deals with a count, Companies, People, Forecast, Reports) and an underline on the current one; content sits in a centred column up to 1200px wide. Content: a heading with an owner filter and an Add deal button; four stat cards (open pipeline, weighted forecast, won this quarter, win rate); a funnel card with one horizontal bar per stage (Lead, Qualified, Proposal, Negotiation, Won) showing count and value, with bar widths animated from the data; an activity feed card beside it; and a deals table (deal, company, value, stage as a select, owner avatar, expected close) where changing the stage recalculates the funnel and the stats. Forecast is value times stage probability (10, 30, 50, 75 and 100 percent). Colours are CSS variables with a dark theme."},
{"id":"messages-app","t":"Messaging app","cat":"Mobile apps","tone":"light","added":"2026-11","d":"A phone-sized chat app: a group conversation with bubbles, a photo, a poll you can vote in, reactions, a typing indicator and replies that arrive after you send, plus the list of chats with unread counts.","how":"Each chat is an array of messages and the thread is drawn from it. Sending pushes your message, shows the typing dots, then pushes a reply after a short wait. The poll stores who voted for what, so the bars and counts are worked out, and the unread badge on the list is the sum over chats.","use":"Messaging and community apps, in-app support chat, marketplace buyer and seller threads, team chat on a phone.","p":"Build a mobile messaging app as one HTML file with no libraries for an app called Chime, shown inside a phone frame on wide screens and full width on phones. Two views. Chats: a title with a new-message button, a search field that filters, and a list of conversations (avatar or stacked avatars for groups, name, last message, time and an unread count badge) and the total unread beside the title. Thread (open on load, for a group called Sunday roast): a header with a back button showing the unread total, the group name and members, and call buttons; then the messages with day separators: incoming bubbles on the left with the sender's name in their own colour and their initials, your bubbles on the right in the accent colour with a delivered tick, a photo message with a caption, and a poll card whose options are buttons showing a bar, the count and who voted, where voting or changing your vote updates it. Each incoming bubble has a small heart button that toggles a reaction count. A composer with an attach button, a growing text field and Send; after sending, show Priya is typing with animated dots, then add a reply. Opening a chat clears its unread count. Colours are CSS variables with a dark theme."},
{"id":"landing-page","t":"SaaS landing page","cat":"Pages","tone":"light","d":"A complete product landing page: sticky nav, hero with a product preview, logo row, feature grid, a three-step how-it-works, testimonials, FAQ and a closing call to action.","how":"Each band is a plain section with its own heading, so the page reads in order without scripts. Small scripts add only what needs them: the nav gains a border once you scroll, sections fade up as they enter the view, the mobile menu toggles and the FAQ uses native details elements.","use":"Product and startup home pages, feature launches, waiting-list pages, internal tool marketing.","p":"Build a SaaS landing page as one HTML file with no libraries. Sticky top nav (logo, four links, Sign in, Start free; collapses to a menu button under 760px with aria-expanded). Hero: an eyebrow pill, a large headline, a line of copy, two buttons, a note under them, and a product preview drawn with HTML and CSS (a small dashboard mock with sidebar, stat tiles and a chart made of bars). A row of five customer names in muted text. Features: six cards with an icon tile, title and two lines. How it works: three numbered steps joined by a line. Testimonials: three quote cards with name and role. FAQ: five native details elements with a rotating marker. Final call to action band in the ink colour with an email field and button. Footer with four link columns. Sections fade and rise in with an IntersectionObserver, disabled under prefers-reduced-motion. Colours and radius are CSS variables with a dark theme; the page is fully responsive."},
{"id":"kanban-board","t":"Kanban board","cat":"Work & projects","tone":"light","d":"A project board with four columns. Drag cards between them, or move them with the keyboard, add a card inline and watch the column counts update.","how":"Cards live in one array with a column field; the board is re-rendered from it after every change. Dragging uses the native drag events with a drop indicator. Every card also has a Move menu and Alt + arrow shortcuts, so the board works without a pointer, and each move is announced.","use":"Task trackers, sales pipelines, hiring, content calendars, support queues.","p":"Build a kanban board as one HTML file with no libraries, inside an app shell (sidebar, top bar, content). Four columns (Backlog, In progress, Review, Done), each a labelled region with a count and an Add card button that reveals an inline input. Cards show a title, a coloured tag, a priority badge, a due date, an assignee avatar and a comment count. Support native drag and drop between and within columns with a visible drop line. For keyboards: each card is focusable; Alt + Left/Right moves it between columns and Alt + Up/Down reorders it; a Move to select on each card does the same. Announce every move in an aria-live region (\"Moved Title to Review, position 2 of 4\"). Keep cards in one array and re-render after each change, restoring focus to the moved card. Columns scroll horizontally on small screens."},
{"id":"inbox","t":"Inbox, three panes","cat":"Communication","tone":"light","d":"A mail client layout: folders, a message list with unread marks and a reading pane, with star, archive, keyboard navigation and a reply box.","how":"Messages are an array; the list and reading pane are rendered from it and a selected id. The list is a listbox, so Up and Down move through messages and opening one marks it read. On a phone the three panes become one, with a Back button to return to the list.","use":"Email, support desks, notifications centres, messaging, ticket queues.","p":"Build a three-pane inbox as one HTML file with no libraries, for a mail product called Postbox. Shell: a 56px icon rail on a dark inverted surface (logo, five icon links with tooltips, theme switch, avatar) and a slim header with a breadcrumb and search. Pane 1: folders (Inbox with unread count, Starred, Sent, Archive) and coloured labels. Pane 2: a message list as role=listbox; each option shows sender avatar, sender, time, subject, a one-line preview, an unread dot and a star toggle; Up/Down arrows move the selection and J/K do too. Pane 3: the open message with subject, sender line, body paragraphs, an attachment chip, Archive / Star / Mark unread buttons and a reply textarea with a Send button that appends the reply to the thread. Opening a message marks it read and updates the folder count; archiving removes it and selects the next. Under 900px show only one pane at a time with a Back to inbox button. Keep data in an array and render from state."},
{"id":"trip-pass","t":"Trip and boarding pass app","cat":"Mobile apps","tone":"light","added":"2026-11","d":"A phone-sized travel app: the day of a trip as a timeline with a live countdown, a boarding pass shaped like a ticket with a barcode, and a packing list that keeps score.","how":"The journey is a list of timed steps; the next one is found by comparing each time with the clock, which also drives the countdown. The ticket's notches are two circles cut into its sides and the barcode is drawn from the booking reference, so a different reference gives different bars. The packing list is an array of ticks with a progress bar.","use":"Airline, rail and event ticket apps, travel itineraries, delivery and appointment trackers, anything with a pass to show at a gate.","p":"Build a mobile trip app as one HTML file with no libraries for a travel app called Passage, shown inside a phone frame on wide screens and full width on phones. Bottom tab bar with Trip, Pass and Packing (role=tablist, arrow keys). Trip: a photograph header with the destination and dates over it, a card that says how long until boarding (a countdown that ticks, based on a fixed demo clock that advances in real time), then the day as a vertical timeline: each step has a time, an icon in a circle, a title and a detail line; past steps are ticked and dimmed, the next one is marked Next. Pass: a boarding pass shaped like a ticket, with the airline and flight on a dark inverted top part, origin and destination codes in very large type joined by a line with a plane, a grid of passenger, date, gate, seat, group and boarding time, then a perforated line with a notch cut into each side and a barcode drawn from the booking reference, with the reference beneath; under it an Add to wallet button and a Brighten screen toggle. Packing: a progress bar with a count, and a checklist grouped into Documents, Clothes and Bits, with an input to add your own item. Colours are CSS variables with a dark theme."},
{"id":"data-table","t":"Customers data table","cat":"Tables & lists","tone":"light","d":"A working data grid: search, status filters, sortable columns, row selection with a bulk action bar, pagination and a detail drawer.","how":"One array of records is filtered, sorted and paged by a single render function, so every control just changes a small state object. Sorting is announced through aria-sort on the header. Selecting rows reveals a bulk bar; opening a row slides in a drawer that traps nothing and closes with Escape.","use":"CRMs, admin panels, user management, order lists, inventory, any list of records.","p":"Build a customers data table as one HTML file with no libraries, inside an app shell (sidebar, top bar, content). Above the table: a heading with a count, a search input, filter chips for status (All, Active, Trial, Churned) and an Add customer button. The table has a select-all checkbox, then Name (avatar, name, email), Company, Plan, Status badge, MRR and Last seen. Column headers are buttons that sort ascending then descending and set aria-sort. Selecting rows shows a bar with the count and Email, Export and Delete actions. Below: rows-per-page info and Previous / Next buttons. Clicking a row opens a right-hand drawer with the customer details and recent activity; Escape or the close button dismisses it and returns focus to the row. Keep 40 generated records in an array and derive the view from a state object {q, status, sort, dir, page, selected}."},
{"id":"pricing-page","t":"Pricing page","cat":"Pages","tone":"light","d":"A pricing page with a monthly and yearly switch that rolls the prices, three plans with one highlighted, a seat slider that recalculates the total, a full comparison table and billing questions.","how":"Prices are numbers in an array; the period switch and the seat slider feed one function that recalculates and writes every figure, announcing the total politely. The comparison table is a real table with row headers, and its header row sticks while you scroll through the features.","use":"SaaS and subscription pricing, plan pickers inside apps, upgrade screens, proposal pages.","p":"Build a pricing page as one HTML file with no libraries. Header with a heading, a line of copy and a Monthly / Yearly switch (role=group, aria-pressed) with a Save 20% tag. Three plan cards (Starter, Team, Business): name, who it is for, price per seat that animates when the period changes, a short feature list with ticks and a button; the middle plan is highlighted with a Most popular badge. Below: a seats slider (1 to 50, with a number output) and a line stating the total per month for each plan at that size, in an aria-live region. Then a comparison table grouped into Core, Collaboration and Security with ticks, dashes and values, a sticky header row and a caption. Then four billing questions as details elements, and a contact band for larger teams. All numbers come from one data object and one recalculation function; yearly shows the monthly equivalent and the amount billed yearly. Responsive: cards stack and the table scrolls sideways inside its own container. Light and dark themes."},
{"id":"ai-chat","t":"AI assistant chat","cat":"Communication","tone":"light","d":"A chat workspace: past conversations, a message thread with a reply that streams in word by word, suggested prompts, a stop button and copy on every answer.","how":"Sending a message appends it to the thread and starts a timer that reveals the canned reply a few words at a time, which is how a streamed answer looks. The thread is a log region, so screen readers hear new messages. Enter sends, Shift + Enter makes a new line, and Stop ends the stream early.","use":"AI assistants, support chat, copilots inside tools, team messaging.","p":"Build an AI chat screen as one HTML file with no libraries, for an assistant called Juniper. Shell: only a slim bar with the logo, a theme switch and the account avatar, so the conversation has the whole window. Left column: a New chat button and a list of past conversations grouped by Today and Earlier. Main column: a scrolling thread with role=log where user messages sit right in a tinted bubble and assistant messages sit left with an avatar, formatted paragraphs and a Copy button; an empty state with four suggested prompt cards; and a composer fixed at the bottom with an auto-growing textarea, a model picker and a Send button that becomes Stop while a reply is streaming. Simulate streaming by revealing a prepared reply a few words every 40ms with a blinking caret, scrolling to the bottom as it grows. Enter sends, Shift+Enter adds a line. With prefers-reduced-motion show the whole reply at once. Hide the conversation list under 900px."},
{"id":"mobile-banking","t":"Mobile banking app","cat":"Mobile apps","tone":"light","d":"A phone-sized banking app with four working tabs: a balance you can hide, cards to swipe between, recent transactions with search, a spending breakdown and a send-money sheet.","how":"The app is a column the width of a phone with a tab bar at the bottom; each tab is a section toggled by one state value. The card stack uses scroll snapping, the spending ring is an SVG drawn from category totals, and the send sheet slides up over the page and traps nothing but returns focus when closed.","use":"Fintech and wallet apps, loyalty and rewards, budgeting tools, any mobile-first product screen.","p":"Build a mobile banking app screen as one HTML file with no libraries, shown inside a phone frame on wide screens and full width on phones. Bottom tab bar with Home, Cards, Insights and Profile (role=tablist, arrow keys). Home: greeting, total balance with a hide or show button, quick actions (Send, Request, Top up, More), and a list of recent transactions grouped by day with icon, name, category and signed amount, plus a search field that filters them. Cards: horizontally scroll-snapping cards with brand, masked number and balance, and controls for freeze (role=switch) and show details. Insights: an SVG donut of spending by category with a legend and a month switch. Profile: a short settings list. Send opens a bottom sheet with a recipient list, an amount keypad and a Send button that shows a success state; Escape and a close button dismiss it and focus returns to the trigger. Keep data in arrays; amounts use tabular numbers; include light and dark themes."},
{"id":"calendar","t":"Calendar","cat":"Work & projects","tone":"light","d":"A month calendar with coloured events, a day panel beside it, month switching and a quick form to add an event.","how":"The month grid is computed from a year and month, so the previous and next buttons just change two numbers. Events are an array keyed by date; clicking a day selects it, fills the side panel and lets you add to it. The grid is a real grid for keyboards, with arrow-key movement between days.","use":"Scheduling tools, booking systems, team planners, content calendars, personal apps.","p":"Build a calendar screen as one HTML file with no libraries, inside an app shell (sidebar, top bar, content). Header: the month and year, previous, next and Today buttons, and a Month / Agenda switch. Month view: a seven-column grid starting on Monday with weekday headers; each day cell is a button showing the date, up to three event chips coloured by calendar and a +n more line; today is ringed and the selected day is filled. Arrow keys move between days, Enter selects. Right panel: the selected date in words, its events as a list with time, title and calendar colour, and a small form (title, time, calendar select) that adds an event. Agenda view lists the next fourteen days that have events. Keep events in an array of {date, time, title, cal} and render from state {year, month, selected, view}. The grid becomes a compact list of days under 700px."},
{"id":"product-page","t":"Shop product page","cat":"Pages","tone":"light","d":"An online shop product page: a gallery with thumbnails, colour and size choices, quantity, an Add to bag button that fills a slide-in bag with a running total, fold-out details and customer reviews.","how":"The product picture is an SVG drawn from the chosen colour, so switching colour repaints the gallery without any image files. The bag is an array of lines; the badge, the drawer list and the subtotal are all drawn from it. Sold-out sizes are real disabled buttons.","use":"Online shops, marketplaces, booking and ticket pages, any page where people choose options and add to a basket.","p":"Build an e-commerce product page as one HTML file with no libraries. Sticky top nav with a logo, three links, a theme button and a Bag button showing an item count. Two columns that stack on phones: left, a gallery with a large picture and four thumbnail buttons (the picture is an inline SVG of the product drawn from the selected colour, with four views); right, a breadcrumb, product name, star rating with review count, price, a colour picker (radio group of swatches), a size picker (radio group of chips, one sold out and disabled), a quantity stepper, an Add to bag button, delivery notes and three native details sections. Add to bag opens a bag drawer from the right (role=dialog, closes on Escape and returns focus) listing lines with quantity steppers, remove buttons, subtotal and a Checkout button. Below: a reviews section with an average, rating distribution bars and three reviews, and a row of four related products. Colours are CSS variables with a dark theme."},
{"id":"file-manager","t":"File manager","cat":"Tables & lists","tone":"light","d":"Folders and files in a grid or a list, with breadcrumbs, multi-select, a details panel and a storage meter.","how":"The tree is a nested object and the current folder is a path array, so breadcrumbs and Back are the same data. One render draws either the grid or the list from the folder's children. Selection is a set of names; one selected item fills the details panel.","use":"Cloud storage, document management, media libraries, asset pickers, admin tools.","p":"Build a file manager screen as one HTML file with no libraries, inside an app shell. Toolbar: breadcrumbs from the current path, a Grid / List switch, sort by name, date or size, and New folder and Upload buttons. Content shows the current folder's children: folders first, then files with a type icon tile (colour by kind: image, video, document, sheet, archive), name, size and modified date. Double-click or Enter opens a folder; single click selects (Ctrl or Shift for multiple) and shows a details panel on the right with a preview tile, kind, size, modified, owner and Share, Download and Delete buttons; a selection bar shows the count. Delete removes selected items with an undo toast. A storage card shows a segmented bar by kind and how much is used. Keep the tree as nested objects and render from state {path, view, sort, selected}. Details panel drops below on small screens."},
{"id":"settings","t":"Settings","cat":"Account & admin","tone":"light","d":"An account settings area with tabbed sections: profile form with validation, notification switches, team members with roles, and a danger zone that asks you to type to confirm.","how":"The sections are real tabs with arrow-key movement. The form tracks whether anything changed and only then shows a sticky Save bar. Switches are buttons with role=switch. Deleting needs the workspace name typed exactly before the button enables.","use":"Account pages, workspace admin, preferences, team management.","p":"Build a settings screen as one HTML file with no libraries, under a slim bar with the logo, a Back to workspace link, a theme switch and the avatar; the settings sit in a centred column up to 960px wide. A vertical tablist (Profile, Notifications, Team, Danger zone) on the left of the content, becoming a horizontal scroller on small screens, with Left/Right/Up/Down arrow keys moving between tabs. Profile: avatar with Change button, name, email (validated on blur with an inline error linked by aria-describedby), role select, bio textarea with a character count, and time zone. Notifications: a table of events against Email and Push, each cell a role=switch button. Team: a list of members with avatar, email, a role select and Remove, plus an Invite by email row. Danger zone: a bordered red card where the Delete workspace button enables only after typing the workspace name. Show a sticky bar with Discard and Save changes only when something has changed; saving shows a status message."},
{"id":"fitness-app","t":"Fitness tracker app","cat":"Mobile apps","tone":"light","d":"A phone-sized fitness app with three tabs: activity rings that fill as you log, a water counter and weekly steps; workouts with a running timer you can pause; and progress with a chart and personal bests.","how":"The rings are SVG circles whose dash length is the share of each goal, with a transition so they sweep when a number changes. The workout timer counts real seconds and fills a ring; finishing a workout adds its minutes and calories to today, so the first tab changes too.","use":"Health and fitness apps, habit trackers, wellbeing and step challenges, any goal-progress screen on a phone.","p":"Build a mobile fitness tracker as one HTML file with no libraries, shown inside a phone frame on wide screens and full width on phones. Bottom tab bar with Today, Workouts and Progress (role=tablist, arrow keys). Today: the date, three concentric SVG activity rings (move calories, exercise minutes, stand hours) with a legend of value against goal, a weekly steps bar chart with today highlighted, and a water tracker of eight glasses you tap to fill. Workouts: a list of four workouts with duration and intensity; choosing one opens a timer view with a large mm:ss readout inside a progress ring, Pause or Resume, and Finish, which adds the minutes and calories to today's totals and returns to the list with a confirmation. Progress: a Week / Month switch over a line of active minutes drawn as an SVG path, and a list of personal bests. Rings animate with a stroke-dasharray transition. Colours are CSS variables with a dark theme."},
{"id":"project-timeline","t":"Project timeline","cat":"Work & projects","tone":"light","d":"A Gantt-style timeline: phases you can fold, task bars with progress, milestones, a today line and a week or day zoom. Pick a task to change its progress.","how":"Every bar is placed from two numbers, a start day and a length, multiplied by one CSS variable for the width of a day. Zooming only changes that variable. The names column is sticky so it stays put while the timeline scrolls sideways.","use":"Project and release planning, agency schedules, roadmaps, construction and event plans.","p":"Build a project timeline (Gantt chart) as one HTML file with no libraries, inside an app shell (sidebar, top bar, content; sidebar becomes a slide-in menu under 860px). A heading with overall progress, a Weeks / Days zoom switch and a Today button. The chart is a horizontally scrolling grid covering eight weeks: a sticky left column of names, a sticky header of week labels (and day numbers when zoomed in), weekly grid lines and a vertical today line. Tasks are grouped into four phases that collapse with a button (aria-expanded). Each task is a button positioned with left and width computed from start day and duration times a --cw CSS variable, with an inner fill for progress; milestones are diamonds. Selecting a task shows a details card with dates, owner, a status badge and a range slider that updates the bar and the overall progress. Colours are CSS variables with a dark theme."},
{"id":"inventory","t":"Product inventory","cat":"Tables & lists","tone":"light","d":"A stock room screen: totals that react, search and category filters, a low-stock switch, stock you adjust in place with plus and minus, a live status for every row and a dialog to add a product.","how":"Products are an array. Status is never stored, it is worked out from the stock number each time the table draws, so pressing plus or minus changes the badge, the low-stock count and the stock value together. The add form is a native dialog element, which handles focus and Escape for you.","use":"Shop back offices, warehouse and stock tools, catalogue admin, any list people edit in place.","p":"Build a product inventory screen as one HTML file with no libraries, for a shop back office called Fernhill: one top bar holding the logo, six navigation links with an underline on the current one, search, a theme switch and the account avatar, with the content in a centred column up to 1200px wide; under 860px the links move to a second, sideways-scrolling row. A heading with an Add product button; four stat cards (products, low stock, out of stock, stock value) computed from the data; a toolbar with a search field, a category select and a Low stock only switch (role=switch); and a table with a colour swatch and name, SKU, category, price, a stock stepper with minus and plus buttons around the number, a status badge derived from stock (In stock, Low under 10, Out at 0) and a Listed switch. Adjusting stock updates the stats at once. Add product opens a native dialog with name, category, price and stock fields and validation, and adds a row. Show an empty state when nothing matches. Colours are CSS variables with a dark theme."},
{"id":"docs-page","t":"Documentation page","cat":"Pages","tone":"light","d":"A developer docs layout: a section menu you can filter, an article with copy buttons on every code block, install tabs, a callout, an On this page list that follows your scroll, and previous and next links.","how":"Three columns that fold down as the screen narrows: the menu becomes a slide-in panel and the On this page list disappears. An IntersectionObserver watches the headings and marks the one you are reading. Copy buttons read the text straight from the code block beside them.","use":"Product and API documentation, help centres, handbooks, changelogs, any long reference page.","p":"Build a documentation page as one HTML file with no libraries. A sticky top bar with a logo, a version badge, a search field that filters the side menu, a theme button and a menu button on small screens. Three columns: a sticky left menu of grouped links with the current page marked (slides in as a panel under 860px), the article, and a sticky right On this page list that highlights the section in view using IntersectionObserver (hidden under 1100px). The article has a breadcrumb, title, lead paragraph, h2 sections, a tabbed install block (npm, pnpm, yarn; role=tablist with arrow keys), dark code blocks each with a Copy button that confirms, an info callout, a parameters table, a Was this helpful? yes or no control that thanks you, and previous and next page cards. Colours are CSS variables with a dark theme; code blocks scroll sideways on their own and the page never does."},
{"id":"sign-in","t":"Sign in and sign up","cat":"Account & admin","tone":"light","d":"A split sign-in screen: a form with validation, show-password, single sign-on buttons and a switch to create an account with a password strength meter.","how":"One form serves both modes; a toggle changes the heading, shows the name field and swaps the button text. Each field validates when you leave it and again on submit, with the message tied to the field. The strength meter scores the password on length and character variety.","use":"Any product with accounts: SaaS, dashboards, mobile web apps, customer portals.","p":"Build a sign-in screen as one HTML file with no libraries. Two columns: a form column and a brand panel with a headline, three short benefit lines and a quote (the panel hides under 820px). The form has a logo, a heading, Continue with Google and Continue with Apple buttons, a divider, then email and password fields with visible labels; password has a Show / Hide button with aria-pressed; a Remember me checkbox and a Forgot password link; a primary submit button. A link switches to Create account mode, which adds a full name field, a strength meter (four segments and a word: weak, fair, good, strong) and a terms checkbox. Validate on blur and on submit: required, email pattern, password at least eight characters; set aria-invalid and show an error linked with aria-describedby; focus the first invalid field. On success show a spinner in the button, then a confirmation message with role=status. Include a light and dark theme toggle."},
{"id":"onboarding","t":"Onboarding wizard","cat":"Account & admin","tone":"light","d":"A four-step set-up flow: choose what you are here for, name the workspace, invite teammates, then a summary. Progress shows at the top and every step can be skipped or revisited.","how":"The steps are sections shown one at a time; a single index decides which is visible, how far the progress bar has filled and what the buttons say. Answers live in one object, and the last step is drawn from it. Moving on places focus on the new step's heading so screen readers follow.","use":"First-run experiences, account set-up, product tours with input, checkout-style flows, surveys.","p":"Build an onboarding wizard as one HTML file with no libraries. A centred card with a progress bar and a Step n of 4 label. Step 1: What brings you here? as a group of large selectable cards (role=radio in a radiogroup, arrow keys move). Step 2: workspace name (required, live preview of the URL slug) and team size as segmented choices. Step 3: invite teammates: an email input that adds chips on Enter or comma, each removable, with validation. Step 4: a summary list of the answers with Edit links back to each step, and a Finish button that shows a success state with a drawn tick. Back and Continue buttons; Skip for now on optional steps; Continue is disabled until required fields are valid. Keep answers in one object, render the summary from it, and move focus to each step's heading on change. Slide steps in the direction of travel; no slide under prefers-reduced-motion. Include a theme toggle."},
{"id":"billing","t":"Billing and usage","cat":"Account & admin","tone":"light","d":"A billing page: the current plan with a plan switcher, usage meters that warn as they fill, the payment method and a table of invoices to download.","how":"Plans are an array; choosing one opens a confirmation panel that states the new price and what changes before anything is applied. Usage bars compute their width and colour from used and limit, and say the numbers in text. Invoices are rows with a status badge.","use":"SaaS account areas, subscription settings, admin consoles, developer platforms with metered usage.","p":"Build a billing and usage screen as one HTML file with no libraries, inside an app shell. Sections: a Current plan card (name, price, renewal date, Change plan button); three plan cards (Starter, Team, Business) with features and a Monthly / Yearly switch that changes prices, the current one marked; choosing another opens an inline confirmation with the price difference and Confirm and Cancel buttons, announced with role=status; Usage this period with four meters (seats, storage, API calls, automation runs) showing used of limit as text and a bar that turns amber above 75 percent and red above 90 percent, each with role=progressbar; a Payment method card with brand, last four digits, expiry and Update; and an Invoices table (date, number, amount, status badge, Download). Keep all values in data objects and re-render on change."}];
